# The business mailbox archive belongs to exactly one host. Terra owns the
# durable mail state, holds the credentials and the borg repository, and is the
# only machine where these units can do anything but fail. On a work laptop
# they would also be plainly inappropriate: company correspondence should not
# be mirrored onto hardware administered by a different organisation.
{{ if ne .chezmoi.hostname "rgam5terra" }}
.config/systemd/user/rg-ttech-backup.service
.config/systemd/user/rg-ttech-backup.timer
.local/bin/rg-ttech-backup
{{ end }}

# Buildbot Terra is headless and does not run the graphical Astroid client.
# Keeping its encrypted desktop config out of the manifest avoids requiring the
# workstation-only age identity during a buildbot apply.
{{ if eq .chezmoi.hostname "rgam5terra" }}
.config/astroid/**
{{ end }}

# The work laptop takes its prose configuration from a branch of the private
# proseguard repo, installed by symlink so a pull is the whole update step.
# Rendering the same files here as well would give two sources of truth, and
# worse: vale loads every directory under StylesPath as a pack, so the
# displaced originals get loaded a second time under a backup name and every
# finding is reported twice.
#
# The rest of this block is the no-secrets-at-rest rule for that host. It asks
# a broker over the tailnet for the one entry it may read, so anything here
# that would place a personal credential or a personal agent profile on
# hardware another organisation administers is excluded rather than encrypted.
{{ if eq .chezmoi.hostname "rgSURFLat" }}
.config/vale/**
.config/proseguard/**
.config/rustical/**
.hermes/**
.omp/agent/AGENTS.md
.omp/agent/config.yml
.omp/mcp.json
# Its template is guarded to the laptop and renders empty here, which chezmoi
# reads as "remove the target". This host runs the service and needs the file,
# so leave it to the machine rather than deleting it on every apply.
.config/orgaw/config.toml
{{ end }}

